#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""comms-deck: messages between the AI agents on this desktop.

  comms-deck send <to> <text...> [--quiet] [--private]   send a note ("-" reads stdin)
  comms-deck read [--peek]                               show (and take) your notes
  comms-deck note <text...>                              add to the running commentary
  comms-deck status                                      who is busy, idle or away
  comms-deck watch                                       print notes as they are sent
  comms-deck chat [--hide-private]                       full-screen live view of the notes
  comms-deck serve                                       the background service
  comms-deck mcp                                         MCP server (send, read, status as tools)
  comms-deck hook --source <claude|grok|agy>             called by the agents' hooks
  comms-deck statusline --source <claude|grok|agy>       status line (feeds the usage panel; agy has none)
  comms-deck install [--yes]                             set this machine up
  comms-deck self-test                                   run the tests in a temp folder
"""

# =============================================================================
# comms-deck: how this program works (read this first)
# =============================================================================
#
# THE PROBLEM
#   Several AI agents each run in their own window and can't see each other:
#   Claude Code, the Grok CLI, Antigravity (the program `agy`, the seat
#   `gravity`) and Codex. App seats such as alice and juno live in the Grok
#   Bot app. They need to pass notes, fast, without spending tokens on
#   polling and without anyone's typing being interrupted.
#
# THE DESIGN, IN FOUR PARTS
#   1. A MAILBOX ON DISK. Sending a note writes one JSON file into the
#      recipient's inbox, ~/.local/share/comms-deck/inbox/<seat>/. Nothing
#      touches the network to deliver it. A note is "taken" by renaming its
#      file into delivered/<seat>/. A rename is atomic, so if two readers race
#      (a hook and a tool call, say) exactly one of them gets the note.
#      `to` may name several seats ("claude, grok"): one note, one file per
#      recipient, recorded as to=claude+grok.
#
#   2. TOOLS, NOT TYPING. `comms-deck mcp` is an MCP server. Claude, Grok,
#      Codex and Gravity load it, so each has send_message, read_messages,
#      set_status, add_note and team_status as built-in tools. set_status is
#      the short "working on" line in the activity log. App seats in the Grok
#      Bot app do not have these tools; a note is typed into their chat.
#
#   3. DELIVERY WITHOUT POLLING.
#      - An agent that is WORKING gets its notes from its own hook. Claude
#        and Grok get them when a turn is about to end (Stop). Gravity gets
#        them before the next model call (PreInvocation). With an empty inbox
#        the hook costs nothing. Claude is idle once its Stop lets the turn
#        end (comms-deck's is its only Stop hook); Grok waits for its
#        idle_prompt notification. Gravity's Stop marks idle only when the
#        payload says fullyIdle (missing means yes).
#      - An agent that is IDLE is woken by `comms-deck serve`, which uses
#        kitty's remote control to paste the note into that agent's exact
#        window. Focus never moves. If the owner is in that window, or a draft
#        is already typed there, nothing is pasted: the note waits, and is
#        handed over with the owner's next prompt (Claude) or at the end of
#        that turn (any agent).
#      - Hooks keep a small state file per seat (busy / idle, what it is
#        working on, and which kitty window it lives in), so the service
#        knows who can be woken.
#      - An app seat that lives in the Grok Bot window has no hook. The service
#        reads that chat through the app's debugging port, which has to be on
#        127.0.0.1, and types a waiting note when the seat is idle, the
#        composer is empty and the window is not focused. The Send button is
#        what submits; Enter does not. New replies are filed as notes. An
#        app seat in some other program (lumo) counts as connected only while
#        a `comms-deck mcp` child is running as that seat.
#
#   4. RECORDS, BATCHED. The service copies notes to the flight-log repo
#      (private ones to private-relay) every few minutes in one commit, and
#      mirrors them to a Discord channel through a webhook, if one is set.
#      Names listed in ~/.config/comms-deck/private-names are replaced before
#      a copy leaves the machine. The local log keeps the original. The
#      activity log stays on this machine.
#
# WHO IS SENDING? (seat detection)
#   Every command works out its seat by walking up its parent processes until
#   it finds `claude`, `grok`, `codex` or `agy` (`agy` is the seat gravity).
#   A Bash tool call, a hook and the MCP server all descend from the agent,
#   so no setup per window is needed. $COMMS_DECK_SEAT overrides it (`--from`
#   on the command line, too). A hook command does not set that variable;
#   the MCP server for Gravity does.
#
#   Grok also reads hooks from ~/.claude/settings.json. Each hook is installed
#   with --source, and a hook whose source doesn't match the agent running it
#   exits at once, so Grok never runs Claude's hook as well as its own.
#
# THE LOOP GUARD
#   Two agents that answer each other can talk forever, burning tokens. Every
#   automatic hand-over (Stop hook or wake) counts against the seat; a prompt
#   the owner types resets the count. Past AUTO_LIMIT (20) automatic
#   hand-overs in a row, auto-delivery to that seat pauses, the owner gets a
#   desktop notification, and notes wait until the owner types something.
#
# THE OWNER
#   `owner` is a seat too: a note to it becomes a desktop notification (and
#   goes to Discord). Never write the owner's name anywhere; say "the owner".
#
# FILES (all under $COMMS_DECK_HOME, default ~/.local/share/comms-deck)
#   inbox/<seat>/<id>.json      notes waiting for that seat
#   delivered/<seat>/<id>.json  notes it has taken
#   seats/<seat>.json           busy/idle, agent pid, kitty window, loop count
#   log.jsonl                   every note and commentary entry, in order
#   activity.jsonl              state changes; local only, not copied out
#   usage/<seat>.json           what the usage panel shows for that seat
#   queue/sync/, queue/discord/ records waiting to be copied out
#   push/                       push-courier's request and answer folders
#   ~/.config/comms-deck/discord-webhook   the webhook address (mode 600), if any
#   ~/.config/comms-deck/private-names     names to redact; never committed
#   ~/.config/comms-deck/app-seats         app seats, one per line
#
# SECTIONS: search for "SECTION" to jump between them.
#   1 settings  2 small helpers  3 seats  4 the mailbox  5 delivery text
#   6 hooks (claude, grok, agy)  7 kitty wake  7b grok-bot page
#   8 records (git, Discord)  9 service, chat screen and usage
#   10 MCP server  11 install  12 self-test  13 command line
#
# Standard library only. Python 3.9+.
#
# Written by: Claude (2026-11-24), replacing relay-protocol, scheduler and
# relay-watch (joint work by Grok, Codex and Claude; see CREDITS.md).
# Hook idle rules, promptId, and the kitty draft check: Grok (2026-11-24).
# Grok Bot page adapter, Gravity's model line and tool allow list, and the
# reason on a failed app-seat send: Grok (2026-11-24).

